import Base
import ../../src/server/server.bend as S
import ../../src/server/kicad.bend as K
import ../../src/core/model.bend as M
import ../../src/core/prompt.bend as Q
import ../../src/core/hub.bend as B
import ../../src/core/bothub.bend as BH
import ../../src/server/botnet.bend as BN

# Snapshot the original Hub.prompt composition from 65605eff.
def cfg() -> S.Cfg:
  S.Cfg{"/tmp/lang-test", "", "127.0.0.1", 3799, "recorded-system", [], "test",
    S.Net{False{}, False{}, "", "", "models", K.Rt{"", False{}, "", ""}, None{}, None{}, None{}, "", None{}, BN.Bn.empty(), False{}, "", ""}}

def before(+c: S.Cfg, +st: M.State) -> String:
  String.join([S.Cfg.prompt(c), S.Hub.prompt.kicad(S.Cfg.rt(c), True{}), S.Hub.prompt.files("/tmp/lang-project", K.Files{"", "", "", ""}),
    Q.Prompt.orchestration(M.Task.may_delegate(B.Task.depth(st, "t")), S.Cfg.mtext(c)), Q.Prompt.background()], "\n\n") ++ S.Hub.prompt.machines(c) ++ BH.Bots.prompt.k(None{}, st, S.Cfg.home(c))

# Recorded by Boat j20261015224814-217597 from before() above.
def recorded() -> String:
  "recorded-system\n\n<kicad_runtime>KiCad is not installed on this machine (no kicad-cli was found). Work on the design files directly, and when a task needs ERC, DRC, exports or another KiCad tool, tell the user KiCad is missing and that they can install it from kicad.org/download or their package manager.</kicad_runtime>\n\n<backplane_project>Project root: `/tmp/lang-project`. No KiCad project was found in this project (its root, or folders up to four deep). When you create one, give the .kicad_pro, .kicad_pcb and .kicad_sch the same name so Backplane picks them up, or name it in `.backplane.json`.\nA KiCad project may sit in a folder below the root. To choose which one is canonical (the one Backplane's viewers, the Open in KiCad button and this list follow), write `.backplane.json` in the project root: `{\"project\": \"<path to the .kicad_pro, from the root>\"}`; its board and schematic are the files beside it with the same name, or name them with the keys `pcb` and `schematic` (paths from the root). Keep any other keys in that file. Backplane reads it at each turn and whenever a viewer opens. To show the user a board, schematic, 3D model or STEP file, call the backplane MCP tool `viewer_show` with its path.</backplane_project>\n\n<orchestration>You may delegate well-scoped, independent pieces of work (footprint or symbol generation, part lookup, ERC/DRC review, mechanical checks) to other models with the backplane MCP tool `delegate_task`; `orchestrator_capabilities` lists them. Models available: models. A child sees only the prompt you give it, so include paths and acceptance criteria, and say whether it may write the canonical board or schematic: only one agent writes a file at a time. Each result reaches you exactly once as a message when the child finishes; end your turn rather than polling. Finished tasks never restart, and delegation depth is limited. For work the user should see as a thread of its own, `thread_launch` starts one in this project, and its reports reach you the same way (each turn end, and at once when it asks the person something); `thread_send` (mode auto, queue or steer), `thread_wait`, `thread_read`, `thread_interrupt` and `thread_update` (rename, or regenerate a title) work on any thread of this project. The person's other machines running Backplane are linked to this one: `machine_list` shows them and their projects; `thread_list` and `thread_launch` take a `machine`, and a thread there has an id like `<link>~<id>` that `thread_send`, `thread_wait`, `thread_read`, `thread_interrupt` and `thread_update` take, so you can start work on another machine or hand it a message. A `>machine:slug` in a message names such a thread.</orchestration>\n\n<background_work>Backplane wakes you itself when an Agent, background shell or monitor task ends. End your turn instead of polling, sleeping or checking in a loop. After a restart you are told what was lost. Start long or detached work with the backplane MCP tool `work_run`, or register what you already started with `work_watch`, then end your turn: Backplane wakes you with the outcome. Heavy jobs that must not run at once (builds, long test runs) share a named lock: start them with `work_run` and `lock: \"<name>\"`, or take it with `lock_acquire` and end your turn (a message tells you when it is yours; `lock_release` after), instead of a bare flock. Never poll or sleep-wait. Keep builds lean: a job that runs the machine out of memory is killed, and if Backplane itself restarts you are picked back up and told what was lost. To use another model, prefer `delegate_task`.</background_work>"
